(min-width: 621px) and (max-width: 620px) are disjoint, so the three-column rule can never win on a narrow window — the exact thing the claim asserts. The one-column rule is also not dead: for any viewport <=620px wide AND taller than 680px (e.g. 600x900), neither line 1224 nor line 1227 matches, so line 804 is the last declaration standing and one column applies. `.glaze-list` grid-template-columns is declared in exactly four places project-wide (477 base, 804, 1224, 1227, confirmed by grep across src/); nothing later reintroduces the conflict, and no JS sets the property inline.

The only case where line 804 is overridden is narrow AND short (<=620px x <=680px), where line 1227 gives two columns — deliberate, documented in the comment at 1210-1218: "620 was written when an item was two lines tall: at 21px each, two columns fit a 240px panel comfortably, and one column of twelve does not fit at all." That is two columns by design, not the three columns the claim describes.

The claim is stale — it describes the pre-split state of code that has already been fixed, and its stated mechanism is arithmetically impossible in the current bytes.

Four rules set .glaze-list columns, all specificity (0,0,1,0), so among matching rules the last in source order wins:
  src/ui/style.css:477   .glaze-list{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;...}   (no MQ)
  src/ui/style.css:803   @media (max-width:620px){
  src/ui/style.css:804     .glaze-list{grid-template-columns:1fr}
  src/ui/style.css:1223  @media (max-height: 680px) and (min-width: 621px){
  src/ui/style.css:1224    .glaze-list{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr}
  src/ui/style.css:1226  @media (max-height: 680px) and (max-width: 620px){
  src/ui/style.css:1227    .glaze-list{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}
No JS sets it: src/ui/hud.js:551 and src/game/game.js:1097 only create the element; no gridTemplateColumns assignment exists in src/.

Truth table (w = viewport width, h = height):
  w>=621, h>680  -> matches 477 only            -> 2 col
  w>=621, h<=680 -> matches 477,1224            -> 3 col
  w<=620, h>680  -> matches 477,804             -> 1 col  (the one-column rule is LIVE)
  w<=620, h<=680 -> matches 477,804,1227        -> 1227 wins -> 2 col

Half 1 of the claim ("a later three-column rule wins on narrow AND short") is false: the three-column rule at 1224 carries `and (min-width: 621px)`, so it cannot match any window <=620px wide. On narrow+short the last matching rule is 1227 (two columns), not 1224.

Half 2 ("the one-column rule is dead") is false: at w<=620 and h>680 — iPhone portrait 390x844, or a desktop window dragged to 600px at 900px tall — rule 804 is the last matching rule and applies. It is overridden only in the narrow+short quadrant, and that override is deliberate: the h<=680 block at 1217-1222 compacts each item to one short line (14px swatch, 11px name, .gt hidden by the rule at 1205-1206, .lock hidden), so two columns is the intended result there.

The file already documents this exact bug as fixed, in the past tense, at src/ui/style.css:1210-1213: "These were one rule, and it sat after the `max-width:620px` one-column rule and beat it — three columns crammed into a panel the earlier rule says has no room for two. A media query adds no specificity; only position does. Split by width so the two cannot fight." The split is present in the bytes at 1223 and 1226.

Only residual, not player-visible: widths strictly between 620 and 621 CSS px (possible under fractional zoom) match neither 804 nor 1227 and fall back to the base two columns. At h<=680 that is identical to the result at exactly 620px; at h>680 it is a half-pixel-wide band showing 2 columns instead of 1. Sub-pixel, unobservable, and not the claimed defect.

The described mechanism is real but the trigger is unreachable, so no player can hit it.

The shared handler at src/game/game.js:428-459 is bound to both events:
  const up = (e) => {
    if (e && e.pointerType === 'touch') { const wasMulti = this._touches.size > 1; onTouchUp(e); if (wasMulti) return; }
    if (e && (e.button === 2 || e.button === 1)) { this.pointer.right = false; ... return; }
    this.pointer.down = false;
    this._onRelease();
  };
  c.addEventListener('pointerup', up);
  c.addEventListener('pointercancel', up);   // line 459
A pointercancel carries button === -1 / buttons === 0, so it misses the ===2/===1 branch and falls through to the down=false line without clearing right. And a stuck right IS loud: game.js:230 gates orbiting purely on it — "if (this.pointer.right) { ... this.eng.rig.orbit(e.movementX ?? 0, e.movementY ?? 0) }" — so every bare mouse move would swing the camera (or spin the pot in GLAZE). So the claim's logic is sound as far as it goes.

But nothing can deliver a mouse pointercancel while the right button is held, on either target platform:

1. Mouse pointercancel in both Blink and WebKit is fired essentially only on native drag-and-drop initiation. That needs draggable content. index.html:16 is a bare `<canvas id="gl"></canvas>` — no draggable attribute — and src/ui/style.css:127 sets `-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none` on the body, so no text-selection drag can begin either. Native drag is also primary(left)-button-initiated; the right button never starts one.
2. The mouse case that would otherwise strand a held button is already handled at game.js:460-462: `window.addEventListener('blur', () => { this.pointer.down = false; this.pointer.right = false; this._touches.clear(); this._pinch = null; })`.
3. The context menu cannot swallow the release: game.js:464, `c.addEventListener('contextmenu', (e) => e.preventDefault())`.
4. Pointer capture is taken for the right button too — game.js:433 `try { c.setPointerCapture?.(e.pointerId); } catch {}` runs BEFORE the `if (e.button === 2 || e.button === 1) { this.pointer.right = true; return; }` at line 434 — and #gl is `position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%` (style.css:136), so a release outside the canvas still targets it.
5. The iPad path never consults e.button. `right` is set in onTouchDown at two fingers (game.js:336) and cleared in onTouchUp whenever `_touches.size < 2` (game.js:406-407), which a touch pointercancel reaches through the same `up` via the pointerType==='touch' branch at 440-443. I walked 2-finger, 3-finger, and simultaneous-cancel orderings: right clears in every one.

The single genuinely reachable mouse pointercancel — a left-button drag-start — is handled correctly by the fall-through, which clears down and calls _onRelease(). Right is false in that case anyway.
